<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Fitzgerald is a 30-year-old dancer and writer, five weeks after her three-month review, who comes for her yearly preventive visit and to talk again about contraception. Her diagnosis was revised at the last visit to bipolar I disorder with psychotic features, now in full remission, on lithium and aripiprazole. She has had no manic, depressive, or psychotic symptoms since the spring. Her periods have been regular since July. She dances about three and a half hours a day, eats three meals and two snacks as she planned with the dietitian, and has gained a little weight, which she accepts. She sleeps eight hours. At the last visit she learned that lithium can affect a developing baby&apos;s heart and that valproate is avoided because of its risks in pregnancy; she does not want a pregnancy for at least two years and would like a long-acting method. She uses condoms with a new partner. Her PHQ-9 today is 3. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visit</td><td>Periodic adult preventive visit, age 30</td></tr><tr><td>Mood and psychosis</td><td>No symptoms since the spring; lithium 300 mg twice daily and aripiprazole 10 mg daily</td></tr><tr><td>Periods</td><td>Regular since July</td></tr><tr><td>Dance and food</td><td>About three and a half hours a day; meals and snacks as planned</td></tr><tr><td>Pregnancy plans</td><td>None for at least two years; would like a long-acting method</td></tr><tr><td>Mood</td><td>PHQ-9 3; item 9 scored 0</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Health risk assessment. Completed by the patient on a tablet in the waiting room and reviewed with her by Dr. Ellery. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Finding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-rated health</td><td>Good</td></tr><tr><td>Psychosocial</td><td>Supported by her roommate, her dance class, and the coordinated specialty care team; writing every morning</td></tr><tr><td>Substance use</td><td>AUDIT-C 1; no drugs</td></tr><tr><td>Tobacco</td><td>Never smoked</td></tr><tr><td>Physical activity</td><td>Dance about three and a half hours a day, with one full rest day a week</td></tr><tr><td>Nutrition</td><td>Three meals and two snacks; calcium-rich foods daily; SCOFF 0 of 5</td></tr><tr><td>Sleep</td><td>About eight hours at regular times</td></tr><tr><td>Sexual and reproductive health</td><td>One new partner; condoms; no pregnancy wanted for at least two years</td></tr><tr><td>Safety at home</td><td>Feels safe; no one hurts, threatens, or controls her; no firearm in the home</td></tr><tr><td>Road safety</td><td>Wears a seat belt</td></tr><tr><td>Activities of daily living</td><td>Independent</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

