  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.5" extension="2015-08-01"/><templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.5.1" extension="2015-08-01"/>
    <code code="11450-4" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Problem List"/>
    <title>Problems</title>
    <text><table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Clinical status</th><th>Verification</th><th>Code</th><th>Provenance</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr ID="problem-1">
    <td>First episode of psychosis with marked mood symptoms: about three weeks of little sleep, rapid speech, racing thoughts, grandiose plans and very long training days, then a voice commenting on her dancing and a belief that she was being watched and tested; tearful low mood since discharge. Diagnosis provisional; differential: bipolar I disorder with psychotic features, schizoaffective disorder (bipolar type), and schizophreniform disorder or schizophrenia, depending on whether the psychosis ever occurred apart from the mood symptoms. Synthetic modern episode linked to her documented first hospitalization</td><td>resolved</td><td>provisional</td>
    <td>F29</td><td>S — Synthetic modern coding of the documented first breakdown. Unspecified psychosis is coded until the course shows how the psychosis relates to the mood symptoms; superseded at the three-month visit.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-2">
    <td>Bipolar I disorder, most recent episode manic with psychotic features, in full remission: the working diagnosis at three months, when her history, her journal, and her roommate&apos;s account (given with her consent) showed that the voice and the belief she was watched began only after weeks of manic symptoms and ended with them, with no psychosis since. Remains provisional: it would be revised to schizoaffective disorder if psychosis ever lasted two weeks or more without a mood episode. Synthetic modern episode linked to the documented breakdown and its retrospective interpretations</td><td>active</td><td>provisional</td>
    <td>F31.74</td><td>S — Synthetic modern diagnosis made from the course; the historical diagnoses stay in the claims.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-3">
    <td>Secondary amenorrhea: no periods for about four months before admission, during very heavy dance training at a low weight (BMI 18.7), with prolactin raised on risperidone; pregnancy test negative</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>N91.1</td><td>S — Synthetic presenting finding linked to her documented training; resolved when her periods returned.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-4">
    <td>Acute viral gastroenteritis (vomiting and watery diarrhea for two days, roommate with the same illness), with mild dehydration; resolved</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>A08.4</td><td>S — Synthetic acute infection; not a historical claim.</td>
  </tr>
  <tr ID="problem-5">
    <td>Lithium level above the therapeutic range (1.4 mmol/L) during dehydration, with nausea and a worse fine tremor, without confusion or unsteadiness; lithium held and restarted; resolved</td><td>resolved</td><td>confirmed</td>
    <td>T43.595A</td><td>S — Synthetic medication adverse effect at a prescribed dose; ICD-10-CM classifies lithium salts with the other antipsychotics and neuroleptics.</td>
  </tr></tbody></table></text>

